Given that \(\mathbf{A}^T = \mathbf{A}^{-1}\), we know that \(\mathbf{A} \mathbf{A}^T = \mathbf{I}\), where \(\mathbf{I}\) is the identity matrix. This gives us the following system of equations:
\[
\begin{pmatrix}
a & b \\
c & d
\end{pmatrix}
\begin{pmatrix}
a & c \\
b & d
\end{pmatrix}
=
\begin{pmatrix}
1 & 0 \\
0 & 1
\end{pmatrix}
.\]
This results in the following equations:
\[
\begin{cases}
a^2 + b^2 = 1 \\
ac + bd = 0 \\
c^2 + d^2 = 1 \\
\end{cases}
.\]
We are asked to find \(a^2 + b^2 + c^2 + d^2\). From the above equations, we have \(a^2 + b^2 = 1\) and \(c^2 + d^2 = 1\). Thus, \(a^2 + b^2 + c^2 + d^2 = 1 + 1 = 2\).
